Minimal repro (/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-tcl-lsp/b4c77bf3-47fd-5ba4-822a-000fa233bcc1/scratchpad/audit/119/minimal/min.tcl):
```
proc A {} {
    oo::define $class method $realmethod {} {return 1}
}
proc B {} {
    oo::define $class method $ensemble {} {return 2}
}
```
`textDocument/documentSymbol` on this file returns proc B's DocumentSymbol with range {start: line 3 char 5, end: line 5 char 0} (correct, matches B's own braces), but nested under it as a child is `Class ${class}` (range line 1, chars 15-21) containing TWO method children: `${realmethod}` (range line 1, chars 29-53 -- physically inside proc A's body, not B's) and `${ensemble}` (range line 4, chars 29-51 -- B's own). So B's symbol subtree includes a child whose range (line 1) falls entirely outside B's own range (lines 3-5) -- a parent/child containment violation that is directly falsifiable from the raw source text with no oracle execution needed. Swapping the class-variable name in B to `$otherclass` (keeping `$realmethod` identical) makes the duplication vanish, and swapping only the class-variable name back while keeping method-variable names different (`$class`/`$realmethod` in A, `$class`/`$ensemble` in B) reproduces it -- proving the merge key is specifically the class-name variable's literal text, not scope. Verified reachable with the real clay.tcl idiom too: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-tcl-lsp/b4c77bf3-47fd-5ba4-822a-000fa233bcc1/scratchpad/audit/119/ensemble_repro.tcl (a faithful extraction of clay.tcl:1185-1232's `Ensemble` keyword + clay.tcl:1922-1932's dispatcher-builder, both of which use `set class [current_class]` then `oo::define $class method $realmethod ...` / `oo::define $class method $ensemble ...`) shows the identical symptom: `InstallEnsembleDispatchers`'s symbol subtree includes a `Method ${realmethod}` child physically located inside the unrelated `Ensemble` proc. tclsh9.0 confirms the repro's actual runtime semantics are correct (`hello from bar` / `hello from baz`), so this is purely an LSP-side document-model defect, not a misunderstanding of Tcl semantics. Separately confirmed as correct/REFUTED: `w1 foo bar` / `w1 foo baz` call sites -- hover and go-to-definition on `w1`, `foo`, and `bar` all correctly return empty/abstain (verified against a baseline plain oo::class case where `p1 greet` DOES resolve hover/definition correctly to `::Plain::greet`), and `textDocument/publishDiagnostics` on the full ensemble_repro.tcl produces only two unrelated W104 hints, no false "unknown method" diagnostics.
